Programming in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>4  Functions</H1>
<HR>
As programs grow, the same few lines tend to turn up again and again. A <I>function</I> gives a piece of code
a name, so you can write it once and use it wherever you need it. In this chapter you will write your own
functions, and use them to make a temperature table and to roll thousands of dice.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Writing a function</H2>
You have been <I>calling</I> functions since chapter 1: <CODE>print</CODE>, <CODE>input</CODE>,
<CODE>Math.round</CODE>. Now you'll write some. This one prints a line of stars:<P>
<PRE>
function stars() {
  print('**********');
}

stars();
print('Hello');
stars();
</PRE>
The word <CODE>function</CODE> is followed by the function's name, a pair of brackets, and a block holding
the statements the function carries out. Writing the function doesn't run it: it only teaches JavaScript a
new word. The statements in the block run each time the function is <I>called</I>, with
<CODE>stars()</CODE>. So this program prints a line of stars, <I>Hello</I>, and another line of stars.<P>
Function names follow the same rules as variable names. A good function name usually starts with a verb,
because a function does something: <CODE>printTitle</CODE>, <CODE>rollDie</CODE>,
<CODE>toFahrenheit</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>Parameters</H2>
A function becomes much more useful when you can give it values to work with. The names in the brackets
are called <I>parameters</I>:<P>
<PRE>
function stars(count) {
  print('*'.repeat(count));
}

stars(3);
stars(10);
</PRE>
When you call <CODE>stars(3)</CODE>, the parameter <CODE>count</CODE> is set to 3 and the block runs;
<CODE>stars(10)</CODE> runs it again with <CODE>count</CODE> set to 10. A value given to a function when you
call it is called an <I>argument</I>.<P>
A function can have several parameters, separated by commas:<P>
<PRE>
function line(character, count) {
  print(character.repeat(count));
}

line('=', 20);
line('-', 5);
</PRE>
The arguments are matched to the parameters in order: the first argument goes to the first parameter, and
so on.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Returning a value</H2>
Some functions do something, like <CODE>print</CODE>. Others work something out and give back an answer,
like <CODE>Math.sqrt</CODE>. To give back an answer, use <CODE>return</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
function toFahrenheit(celsius) {
  return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
}

print(toFahrenheit(100));          // 212
const warm = toFahrenheit(25);
print(`25C is ${warm}F`);          // 25C is 77F
</PRE>
A call to a function that returns a value can be used anywhere you could use the value itself: in a
calculation, in a template string, or to give a variable its value.<P>
<CODE>return</CODE> also ends the function at once, even if there are more statements after it. That is
useful when a function has to choose its answer:<P>
<PRE>
function describe(celsius) {
  if (celsius &lt;= 0) {
    return 'freezing';
  } else if (celsius &lt; 15) {
    return 'cold';
  } else if (celsius &lt; 25) {
    return 'warm';
  }
  return 'hot';
}
</PRE>
If the temperature is 0 or less, the first <CODE>return</CODE> gives back <I>freezing</I> and nothing else
in the function runs. The last line is only reached if none of the comparisons was true.<P>
A function that finishes without a <CODE>return</CODE> gives back <CODE>undefined</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>A temperature table</H2>
This program uses three functions: two to convert temperatures each way, and one to describe them.<P>
<PRE>
// Convert - temperatures in Celsius and Fahrenheit
function toFahrenheit(celsius) {
  return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32;
}

function toCelsius(fahrenheit) {
  return (fahrenheit - 32) * 5 / 9;
}

function describe(celsius) {
  if (celsius &lt;= 0) {
    return 'freezing';
  } else if (celsius &lt; 15) {
    return 'cold';
  } else if (celsius &lt; 25) {
    return 'warm';
  }
  return 'hot';
}

print('Temperature table');
for (let c = -10; c &lt;= 40; c = c + 10) {
  print(`${c}C = ${toFahrenheit(c)}F (${describe(c)})`);
}

print('');
const f = Number(await input('A temperature in Fahrenheit: '));
const inC = toCelsius(f);
print(`${f}F is ${inC.toFixed(1)}C, which is ${describe(inC)}.`);
</PRE>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Convert" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
Look how easy the functions make the rest of the program to read. The loop says, in effect, "for each
temperature from -10 to 40, print it, what it is in Fahrenheit, and what it feels like". The details of
the arithmetic are tucked away in the functions, where they only have to be got right once.<P>
The loop goes up in tens: <CODE>c = c + 10</CODE> is done after each time round, instead of
<CODE>c++</CODE>. And the last line calls <CODE>describe</CODE> with a temperature that has been converted
from Fahrenheit, so one function's answer is passed straight to another.<P>
Notice, too, that the program is written in two halves: first the functions, then the statements that use
them. That is the usual layout.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Local variables</H2>
Variables made inside a function, and its parameters, belong to that function. They are made afresh each
time the function is called, and disappear when it finishes. They are called <I>local variables</I>:<P>
<PRE>
function double(n) {
  const answer = n * 2;
  return answer;
}

print(double(4));   // 8
print(answer);      // error: answer is not defined
</PRE>
Outside the function, <CODE>answer</CODE> doesn't exist. That is a good thing: it means you can use any
names you like inside a function without worrying whether the rest of the program uses them too. Two
different functions can both have a variable called <CODE>count</CODE>, and they won't interfere.<P>
The part of a program where a variable can be used is called its <I>scope</I>. A variable made inside a
block - a function, an <CODE>if</CODE> or a loop - can be used inside that block, including in blocks inside
it. A variable made outside every block, at the top level of the program, can be used anywhere, including
inside functions.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Functions that call functions</H2>
Functions can call other functions, and that is how big programs are built: out of small functions, each
doing one job well. This program rolls dice:<P>
<PRE>
// Dice - rolling dice, a thousand times at a time
function rollDie() {
  return Math.floor(Math.random() * 6) + 1;
}

function rollTwo() {
  return rollDie() + rollDie();
}

function countTotal(total, times) {
  let count = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; times; i++) {
    if (rollTwo() === total) {
      count = count + 1;
    }
  }
  return count;
}

function twoWide(n) {
  if (n &lt; 10) {
    return ' ' + n;
  }
  return String(n);
}
</PRE>
<CODE>rollDie</CODE> rolls one die, using the random number recipe from chapter 3. <CODE>rollTwo</CODE>
rolls two by calling <CODE>rollDie</CODE> twice. <CODE>countTotal</CODE> throws two dice many times and
counts how often they add up to a given total. <CODE>twoWide</CODE> turns a number into a string two
characters wide, by putting a space in front of numbers under 10, so that the columns line up.<P>
Now the main part of the program:<P>
<PRE>
print('Totals of two dice in 1000 throws:');
for (let total = 2; total &lt;= 12; total++) {
  const count = countTotal(total, 1000);
  const bar = '*'.repeat(Math.round(count / 5));
  print(`${twoWide(total)} ${bar} ${count}`);
}
</PRE>
For each total from 2 to 12, it throws 1000 pairs of dice and prints a row of stars, one for every five
throws that gave that total, followed by the count itself:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/Dice" WIDTH=586 HEIGHT=349></CENTER>
Every time you run it you get slightly different numbers, but always the same shape: 7 is the most likely
total, because there are more ways of making it (1 and 6, 2 and 5, 3 and 4, and the other way round) than
any other.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Arrow functions</H2>
There is a second, shorter way to write a function, called an <I>arrow function</I>:<P>
<PRE>
const double = (n) =&gt; n * 2;
print(double(4));   // 8
</PRE>
The parameters go in brackets, followed by <CODE>=&gt;</CODE> (an equals sign and a greater-than sign, which
together look like an arrow) and then the value to give back. There's no need for <CODE>return</CODE>: a
short arrow function gives back the value after the arrow. The function is then kept in a variable, like
any other value.<P>
When an arrow function needs more than one statement, give it a block, and use <CODE>return</CODE> as
usual:<P>
<PRE>
const describe = (celsius) =&gt; {
  if (celsius &lt;= 0) {
    return 'freezing';
  }
  return 'not freezing';
};
</PRE>
A function with no parameters has an empty pair of brackets: <CODE>() =&gt; print('Hello')</CODE>.<P>
Arrow functions are most useful for small functions that are only needed in one place, as you'll see next.
This book uses <CODE>function</CODE> for the main functions in a program and arrow functions for small
ones.<P>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Passing a function to a function</H2>
A function is a value, just as a number or a string is. You can keep it in a variable, and - this is the
important part - you can give it to another function as an argument. Here is the rest of the Dice program:<P>
<PRE>
function average(roll, times) {
  let sum = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; times; i++) {
    sum = sum + roll();
  }
  return sum / times;
}

const roll20 = () =&gt; Math.floor(Math.random() * 20) + 1;
const one = average(rollDie, 1000);
const two = average(rollTwo, 1000);
const twenty = average(roll20, 1000);
print(`Average of one die: ${one.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Average of two dice: ${two.toFixed(2)}`);
print(`Average of a 20-sided die: ${twenty.toFixed(2)}`);
</PRE>
<CODE>average</CODE> works out the average score of any kind of dice roll. Its first parameter,
<CODE>roll</CODE>, is a function, and <CODE>average</CODE> calls it <CODE>times</CODE> times, adding up the
results. It doesn't know or care what kind of roll it is.<P>
So <CODE>average(rollDie, 1000)</CODE> finds the average of 1000 rolls of one die, and
<CODE>average(rollTwo, 1000)</CODE> of two dice. Notice that <CODE>rollDie</CODE> has no brackets here:
you aren't calling it yourself, you are handing the function over for <CODE>average</CODE> to call.<P>
<CODE>roll20</CODE> is an arrow function that rolls a 20-sided die, written just for the occasion.<P>
This idea - giving a function to another function, which calls it when it needs to - is at the heart of
desktop programming. From chapter 6 onwards, you'll give functions to windows, to say what should happen when
someone clicks on them or presses a key.<P>
<B>Note</B>: if a function uses <CODE>await</CODE> - to ask a question with <CODE>input</CODE>, for example -
you have to put the word <CODE>async</CODE> in front of it, and call it with <CODE>await</CODE> too:
<PRE>
async function askNumber(question) {
  return Number(await input(question));
}

const age = await askNumber('How old are you? ');
</PRE>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI><CODE>function name(parameters) { ... }</CODE> gives a block of code a name. Calling
<CODE>name(arguments)</CODE> runs it, with the parameters set to the arguments.<P>
<LI><CODE>return value</CODE> gives back a value and ends the function.<P>
<LI>Variables made inside a function are local: they only exist inside it.<P>
<LI>Build programs from small functions that each do one job, and call each other.<P>
<LI>An arrow function, <CODE>(x) =&gt; x * 2</CODE>, is a short way of writing a function.<P>
<LI>Functions are values: they can be kept in variables and passed to other functions.<P>
<LI>A function that uses <CODE>await</CODE> must be <CODE>async</CODE>, and called with
<CODE>await</CODE>.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Write a function <CODE>square(n)</CODE> that returns n times n, and use it to print the squares of
the numbers from 1 to 10.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Write a function <CODE>biggest(a, b, c)</CODE> that returns the largest of three numbers, without
using <CODE>Math.max</CODE>.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Write a function <CODE>box(width, height)</CODE> that prints a rectangle of <CODE>#</CODE>
characters, and use it to draw boxes of different sizes.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Add a function <CODE>rollThree</CODE> to Dice that rolls three dice, and print the average of
1000 rolls of it using <CODE>average</CODE>. What do you expect it to be?<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1
<PRE>
function square(n) {
  return n * n;
}

for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {
  print(`${i} squared is ${square(i)}`);
}
</PRE>
<DT><DD>2  Keep the largest so far, and compare each number with it:
<PRE>
function biggest(a, b, c) {
  let largest = a;
  if (b &gt; largest) {
    largest = b;
  }
  if (c &gt; largest) {
    largest = c;
  }
  return largest;
}
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3
<PRE>
function box(width, height) {
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= height; i++) {
    print('#'.repeat(width));
  }
}

box(10, 3);
box(4, 4);
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  About 10.5: the average of one die is 3.5, and three dice give three times as much.
<PRE>
function rollThree() {
  return rollDie() + rollDie() + rollDie();
}
const three = average(rollThree, 1000);
print(`Average of three dice: ${three.toFixed(2)}`);
</PRE>
</DL>

